+# Performance report: BlockInfo (block counts panel and hover highlight)
+
+**Workload**: a client MicroProfiler capture with about 6,000 placed blocks (your PC), and the
+same build on your phone. An empty baseplate was fine on both. The "after" numbers below have
+not been measured yet: this change was made without Studio access, so the before and after
+capture is the first item under "For you to verify".
+
+**Bottleneck**: Luau script time and garbage collection in `BlockInfo.client.luau`, on
+`RenderStepped`. The capture shows `Script_BlockInfo` at about 2.5 ms every frame, with a GC label
+right after it on most frames. On a phone, which runs Luau several times slower than a desktop,
+this one script takes a large share of a frame (about 28.6 ms at 35 FPS).
+
+## What was costing time
+
+Every frame, the script did work that grew with the number of blocks in `Workspace.Blocks`:
+
+1. **`blocksFolder:GetChildren()` three times per frame**: once to count blocks by type, once for
+   the "Total" line, and once to search for the hovered block. Each call builds a new array with
+   one entry per block. At 6,000 blocks that is about 18,000 array slots (roughly 150 KB) of new
+   garbage every frame. That garbage is why the GC label shows up right after the script.
+2. **Reading `block.Name` on every block every frame** to count by type. Each read crosses from
+   Luau into the engine and returns a string, which costs far more than a Luau table lookup.
+3. **A linear hover search**: after the raycast, the script checked the distance from the hit
+   point to every block (`IsA` plus a `Position` read per block, both engine calls) to find the
+   nearest center within 4 studs.
+4. **Rebuilding the label text every frame** by string concatenation (about 8 new strings a
+   frame) and writing `countsLabel.Text` every frame, even when nothing had changed. A new
+   `RaycastParams` was also created every frame.
+
+Items 1 to 3 grow with build size, so the cost would keep climbing past 6,000 blocks. At
+50,000 blocks the old script would take roughly 20 ms per frame on your PC alone.
+
+## What changed
+
+All of it is in `src/StarterPlayer/StarterPlayerScripts/BlockInfo.client.luau`, highest level
+first:
+
+- **Counts are kept up to date from events instead of recounted each frame.** The script counts
+  the existing children once at startup, then updates per-type counts and the total from
+  `Blocks.ChildAdded` and `ChildRemoved`. A placement now costs O(1) once, not O(N) every frame.
+- **The hover search uses a grid index.** Each block is filed under its grid cell (blocks are
+  anchored and snapped to the 4-stud grid by `BlockPlacement.server.luau`) when it arrives. The
+  hover search checks only the cells that could hold a center within `HOVER_RADIUS` of the
+  raycast hit: at most 27 table lookups, whatever the build size. The rule is unchanged: the
+  nearest block center strictly within 4 studs of the hit point. So hovering the ground right
+  next to a block still highlights it, as before.
+- **The label is rebuilt and written only when its content changes**: when a count changes, or
+  when the hovered block changes. The selection box `Adornee` is also written only on a change.
+  `RaycastParams` is created once, and its character filter is updated on `CharacterAdded`.
+- The per-frame work is wrapped in `debug.profilebegin("BlockInfo:Step")` so it has a label of
+  its own in the MicroProfiler.
+
+What is left per frame: one raycast, up to 27 table lookups, and one `Name` read on the hovered
+block. None of it depends on build size, and it allocates almost nothing, so this script should
+stop triggering GC.
+
+## Behavior
+
+Unchanged for players: the same panel, the same lines in the same order, the same counts and
+total, the same highlight and "Pointing at" line. The panel updates on the frame after a block
+is added or removed, just as before.
+
+Two edge cases now work differently, and neither can happen with the current server code:
+
+- Counts follow a block's name when it is added. If something renamed a block that was already
+  placed, the old script would recount it under the new name and the new one does not.
+  `BlockPlacement` never renames blocks.
+- A block's grid cell is read once when it arrives. If something moved a placed block, the
+  hover search could miss it. Blocks are anchored and never moved.
+
+If a future feature renames or moves placed blocks, it must also update this index, or remove
+the block and add it again.
+
+## Results
+
+| Metric | Before | After |
+|---|---|---|
+| `Script_BlockInfo` / `BlockInfo:Step`, PC, ~6,000 blocks | ~2.5 ms every frame | not measured yet (expected well under 0.1 ms) |
+| GC label after the script | most frames | not measured yet (expected gone) |
+| Phone FPS, same build | ~35 | not measured yet |
+| Cost growth with block count | linear, every frame | flat per frame; O(1) per placement |
+
+**Tried and reverted**: nothing. No Studio session was available, so nothing could be measured
+during the change.
+
+**Left alone**: the panel already has its own small ScreenGui, so a text change redraws only
+that ScreenGui. The single raycast per frame is cheap and needed to follow the mouse.
+
+## For you to verify in Studio
+
+1. **Same workload, before and after.** Load (or script) the same build of about 6,000 blocks
+   and stand in the same spot. Take a client MicroProfiler capture with the mouse over the build
+   (Ctrl+F6, then pause, or Ctrl+P to dump). Compare against your 2.5 ms baseline. The script's
+   time now shows under `BlockInfo:Step`. Check that the GC label no longer follows it on most
+   frames.
+2. **Scale test.** Repeat at about 2x and 5x the blocks (12,000 and 30,000). `BlockInfo:Step`
+   should stay about the same size. If it grows, something still scales with the build.
+3. **Behavior check, by hand** (no spec covers this script):
+   - The counts per type and the total match what you placed, including blocks that were already
+     there when you joined (join a server that already has a build).
+   - Hovering a block highlights it and shows "Pointing at: <type>". Hovering the ground right
+     next to a block still highlights that block. Hovering empty sky or open ground clears both.
+   - Hovering through your own character still works, including after you respawn.
+   - A Window (glass) block highlights like the others.
+4. **Phone.** Publish and play the same build on your phone, and check FPS with the Developer
+   Console or the performance stats. Studio numbers come from a desktop, so the phone result is
+   the one that confirms the fix. If the phone is still under target, take a MicroProfiler dump
+   on the phone. The next cost is probably rendering thousands of separate parts, which this
+   script does not control.
